// Native CLI-contract fixture (Severed Chains Native, no upstream origin).
// NOT oracle-derived: this pins the native exit-status contract, not reference output.
//
// `include missing_lib.fate;` names a file that exists nowhere, and it is deliberately
// the FIRST line of executable text so that everything below it is "a statement after a
// failed include". An unresolvable include is fatal (owner ruling 2026-10-28): the run
// stops there with exit 2 and writes no output file, so `helper` is never reached and no
// `unknown function` line is printed. With a `-L` path that is not a directory it stops
// even earlier, before the program text is discussed.
//
// The file still has to be otherwise compilable. That is what makes the check mean
// something: the same source compiles, emits and answers 3 when the include resolves, so
// the 2 here is about the missing file and not about a source the tool cannot parse.
include missing_lib.fate;
def f(a) {
  helper(a);
  return;
}
